    "question": {
      ":question/id": "37dad1eb-14f7-54e9-8854-60a301208bd1",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $a_{21}=-6$ is in the same column as the leading entry $a_{11}=6$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $b_{33}=7$ is to the left of the leading entry $b_{24}=1$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 4 & -9 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -9 & 6 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is in row echelon form.",
