    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e7f5d197-a1ce-5be2-a39c-cac4e783bd77",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $a_{21}=6$ is to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}=-6$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 & 4 & -6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & -1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
